PARKED PATCH - not applied by the build, not in the lunasvg fork (github.com/gereoffy/lunasvg).
For third_party/lunasvg (plutovg/source/plutovg-ft-raster.c), made against the fork's 4c7ed7a on 2026-11-23.

Why it was made
---------------
A review of nitropdf drew a page of 50 000 long lines one across the other, each its own "m l S". nitropdf merged a
thousand of them into one path (as it merges a CAD plot's lines), and the page took 12 s at 900 pixels where the same
lines drawn one by one took 0.7 s. A profile had 93 % of the time in gray_find_cell.

plutovg's rasteriser keeps the cells of a row of pixels in a list sorted by x. Since the fork's fa64290 a cell is looked
up from the cell the row used last, forwards or backwards: that is the next cell for an edge that follows the last one
(a contour, a hatching's lines in their order, an isometric grid), which is what fa64290 was made for. It does not help
when the lines of one path cross each other in any order: every line's cell in a row is somewhere else than the last
line's, so the search walks about a third of the row for each of them, and the time grows with the square of the lines
in a row again.

What it does
------------
A search that has walked 16 cells (PVG_FT_FAR_STEPS) starts again from the cell such a search found last in the same
thirty-second of the row's width (PVG_FT_ROW_BUCKETS), or in the nearest part before it that has one. A row's table of
those 32 cells is taken from the cells' pool at the row's first far search, so a row without one costs nothing, and only
outlines of 1024 points or more (PVG_FT_BUCKET_POINTS) have the rows' table pointers at all. Cells found within a few
steps are found exactly as before, small outlines (a glyph, a single line) are rasterised exactly as before, and the
spans that come out are the same in every case: only where the search starts changes, not what it finds.

Measured (nitropdf without its own merge limit, the 50 000 lines):  900 px 12.3 s -> 2.9 s,  3000 px 53.6 s -> 9.4 s.
One path of the file's own with 20 000 crossing lines ("m l m l ... S"):  900 px 6.1 s -> 0.6 s,  3000 px 97 s -> 5.1 s;
with 2000 lines:  900 px 0.56 s -> 0.09 s,  3000 px 4.1 s -> 0.5 s.

Why it is not used
------------------
No real drawing needs it. Of about 3500 sample drawings (1031 PDF, 1386 draw.io, 1126 DXF / DWG / CorelDRAW / SVG /
WMF / Visio) none renders measurably faster with it (the 1126: 138.6 s without, 138.0 s with), because none has a path
of many long lines crossing in any order. The case the review found is handled in nitropdf itself since d9b030a
(strokes are merged only while they cross a row of pixels 16 times on average: that page takes 1.3 s, with or without
this patch). What is left is a single path that a file itself gives with thousands of crossing lines (a wireframe, a
scribble): synthetic so far. 79 lines in the rasteriser's hottest function are not worth carrying in the fork for that.

When to take it out again: a real drawing that spends its render time in gray_find_cell / gray_record_cell (a long
render of one or a few large paths; sample it with a profiler). Then apply this, measure that file, and repeat the
checks below before it goes into the fork.

How it was checked (the tools are in samples_pdf-test/raster-far-cells/, which is not in git: README.txt there)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- rtest.c, 8 seeds x 150 random paths (crossing lines, short lines, hatchings, polygons, with and without a clip,
  canvases of 7 to 6000 pixels): the same pixels as the unpatched rasteriser; clean under ASan + UBSan.
- The render hashes of the 3500 sample drawings above: identical with and without it.
- Speed on real drawings: within 1-2 % either way (the noise of the measurement). Two earlier forms were slower by
  5-7 % on large CAD drawings (a table of starting points written at every cell; one cleared for every row before the
  band is drawn), which is why the table is taken only when a row first needs it.
- Both rasteriser objects were compiled with one command line (the Makefile's flags) and linked before
  build/libnitrovec.a, so that only this file differed between the two programs compared.

To apply: cd third_party/lunasvg && patch -p1 < ../../patches/parked/plutovg-raster-far-cells.patch
(or git apply; not git am: this text comes before the mail header). The fork commit it was, 655bafd, follows as made.

From 655bafde6a68fda2791c1dabcca4dee07740b845 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: A'rpi <arpi.git@interoot.hu>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 19:04:50 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] plutovg: a scanline's cell far from the one used last looked
 up from its part of the row

A row's cells are a list sorted by x, a cell looked up from the one the row used last. That is
the next cell for an edge that follows the last one, but not for lines drawn across each other
in any order: every line's cell in a row is somewhere else than the last line's, and a path of
a thousand such lines walked a third of the row for each of them (50 000 long lines on a page,
merged a thousand to a stroke: 12 s at 900 pixels, 54 s at 3000).

A search that has walked 16 cells now starts again from the cell such a search found last in
the same thirty-second of the row's width, or in the nearest part before it that has one. A
row's table of those 32 cells is taken from the cells' pool at its first far search, so a row
without one costs nothing, and only outlines of 1024 points or more have the tables' pointers
at all: the cells found within a few steps, and small outlines, are rasterized as before. The
page above takes 2.9 s and 9.4 s.

The spans are the same as before (1200 random outlines against the old code, strokes, fills,
hatchings, with and without a clip, also under ASan and UBSan; 3500 PDF, draw.io, DXF, DWG,
CorelDRAW, SVG, WMF and Visio drawings rendered in full: identical). Their time is unchanged
(138.6 s and 138.0 s for the 1126 drawings other than PDF).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 plutovg/source/plutovg-ft-raster.c | 86 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 79 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/plutovg/source/plutovg-ft-raster.c b/plutovg/source/plutovg-ft-raster.c
index 491b590..740a84b 100644
--- a/plutovg/source/plutovg-ft-raster.c
+++ b/plutovg/source/plutovg-ft-raster.c
@@ -148,6 +148,22 @@ PVG_FT_END_STMNT
   /* maximal number of gray spans in a call to the span callback */
 #define PVG_FT_MAX_GRAY_SPANS  256
 
+  /* A row's cells are a list sorted by x, a cell looked for from the one  */
+  /* used last in the row. That is the next one for an edge that follows   */
+  /* the last (a contour, a hatching's lines in their order), but far away */
+  /* for lines drawn across each other in any order: a thousand of them in */
+  /* one path walked a third of the row for each of their cells. So in an  */
+  /* outline of at least PVG_FT_BUCKET_POINTS points a search that has     */
+  /* walked PVG_FT_FAR_STEPS cells starts again, from the cell such a      */
+  /* search found last in the same part of the row's width (or the nearest */
+  /* part before it that has one); a row has PVG_FT_ROW_BUCKETS parts, its */
+  /* table taken from the cells' pool at its first such search. The cells  */
+  /* found within a few steps are found as before, a row with no far cell  */
+  /* has no table, and a small outline (a glyph, a line) none at all.      */
+#define PVG_FT_ROW_BUCKETS     32
+#define PVG_FT_BUCKET_POINTS   1024
+#define PVG_FT_FAR_STEPS       16
+
 
   typedef struct TCell_*  PCell;
 
@@ -202,6 +218,11 @@ PVG_FT_END_STMNT
 
     PCell*     ycells;
     PCell*     yhints;   /* each row's cell used last */
+    PCell**    ybuckets; /* and, for an outline of many points, each row's     */
+                         /* table of a cell in each of its PVG_FT_ROW_BUCKETS  */
+                         /* parts (see there), NULL until the row needs one    */
+    int        buckets;  /* PVG_FT_ROW_BUCKETS, or 0: no tables                */
+    int        xshift;   /* a cell's part: ( x + 1 ) >> xshift                 */
     TPos       ycount;
   } TWorker, *PWorker;
 
@@ -219,6 +240,7 @@ PVG_FT_END_STMNT
 
     ras.ycells      = (PCell*) buffer;
     ras.yhints      = NULL;
+    ras.ybuckets    = NULL;
     ras.cells       = NULL;
     ras.max_cells   = 0;
     ras.num_cells   = 0;
@@ -281,6 +303,7 @@ PVG_FT_END_STMNT
   {
     PCell  cell, prev, hint;
     TPos    x = ras.ex;
+    int     steps = 0, far = 0;
 
 
     if ( x > ras.count_ex )
@@ -291,6 +314,8 @@ PVG_FT_END_STMNT
     /* each other, and searching from the row's start took the square of */
     /* the cells in a row (long lines, dense hatchings and grids)        */
     hint = ras.yhints[ras.ey];
+
+  Search:
     if ( hint && hint->x > x )
     {
       cell = hint;
@@ -299,6 +324,8 @@ PVG_FT_END_STMNT
       {
         cell = prev;
         prev = prev->prev;
+        if ( ++steps == PVG_FT_FAR_STEPS && ras.buckets && !far )
+          goto Far;
       }
       if ( prev && prev->x == x )
       {
@@ -314,6 +341,8 @@ PVG_FT_END_STMNT
       {
         prev = cell;
         cell = cell->next;
+        if ( ++steps == PVG_FT_FAR_STEPS && ras.buckets && !far )
+          goto Far;
       }
       if ( cell && cell->x == x )
         goto Exit;
@@ -344,7 +373,39 @@ PVG_FT_END_STMNT
 
   Exit:
     ras.yhints[ras.ey] = cell;
+    if ( far && ras.ybuckets[ras.ey] )
+      ras.ybuckets[ras.ey][( x + 1 ) >> ras.xshift] = cell;
     return cell;
+
+  Far:
+    /* far from the row's last cell: from its part's cell, any cell of the */
+    /* row a right place to start from (none there: from the last again)   */
+    {
+      const int  block = (int)( ( PVG_FT_ROW_BUCKETS * sizeof ( PCell ) + sizeof ( TCell ) - 1 ) / sizeof ( TCell ) );
+      PCell*     row   = ras.ybuckets[ras.ey];
+      int        b     = (int)( ( x + 1 ) >> ras.xshift );
+
+
+      if ( !row && ras.num_cells + block <= ras.max_cells )
+      {
+        /* (the row's table: as many cells of the pool as it takes) */
+        row = (PCell*)( ras.cells + ras.num_cells );
+        ras.num_cells += block;
+        for ( b = 0; b < PVG_FT_ROW_BUCKETS; b++ )
+          row[b] = NULL;
+        ras.ybuckets[ras.ey] = row;
+        b = (int)( ( x + 1 ) >> ras.xshift );
+      }
+      if ( row )
+      {
+        while ( b > 0 && !row[b] )
+          b--;
+        if ( row[b] )
+          hint = row[b];
+      }
+      far = 1;
+      goto Search;
+    }
   }
 
 
@@ -1767,6 +1828,11 @@ void PVG_FT_Outline_Get_CBox(const PVG_FT_Outline* outline, PVG_FT_BBox* acbox)
     ras.count_ex = ras.max_ex - ras.min_ex;
     ras.count_ey = ras.max_ey - ras.min_ey;
 
+    ras.buckets = ras.outline.n_points >= PVG_FT_BUCKET_POINTS ? PVG_FT_ROW_BUCKETS : 0;
+    ras.xshift  = 0;
+    while ( ( ( ras.count_ex + 1 ) >> ras.xshift ) >= PVG_FT_ROW_BUCKETS )
+      ras.xshift++;
+
     /* set up vertical bands */
     num_bands = (int)( ( ras.max_ey - ras.min_ey ) / ras.band_size );
     if ( num_bands == 0 )
@@ -1800,11 +1866,12 @@ void PVG_FT_Outline_Get_CBox(const PVG_FT_Outline* outline, PVG_FT_BBox* acbox)
           int    cell_start, cell_end, cell_mod;
 
 
-          ras.ycells = (PCell*)ras.buffer;
-          ras.ycount = band->max - band->min;
-          ras.yhints = ras.ycells + ras.ycount;
+          ras.ycells   = (PCell*)ras.buffer;
+          ras.ycount   = band->max - band->min;
+          ras.yhints   = ras.ycells + ras.ycount;
+          ras.ybuckets = (PCell**)( ras.yhints + ras.ycount );
 
-          cell_start = 2 * sizeof ( PCell ) * ras.ycount;
+          cell_start = ( ras.buckets ? 3 : 2 ) * sizeof ( PCell ) * ras.ycount;
           cell_mod   = cell_start % sizeof ( TCell );
           if ( cell_mod > 0 )
             cell_start += sizeof ( TCell ) - cell_mod;
@@ -1821,8 +1888,8 @@ void PVG_FT_Outline_Get_CBox(const PVG_FT_Outline* outline, PVG_FT_BBox* acbox)
           if ( ras.max_cells < 2 )
             goto ReduceBands;
 
-          for ( yindex = 0; yindex < 2 * ras.ycount; yindex++ )
-            ras.ycells[yindex] = NULL;   /* (and the hints) */
+          for ( yindex = 0; yindex < ( ras.buckets ? 3 : 2 ) * ras.ycount; yindex++ )
+            ras.ycells[yindex] = NULL;   /* (and the hints, the rows' tables) */
         }
 
         ras.num_cells = 0;
@@ -1993,7 +2060,12 @@ void PVG_FT_Outline_Get_CBox(const PVG_FT_Outline* outline, PVG_FT_BBox* acbox)
     if ( cells > area )
       cells = area;
     cells += (double)( ymax - ymin );   /* (those left of the clip box, one a row) */
-    return (size_t)fmin( cells * sizeof ( TCell ) + 2.0 * sizeof ( PCell ) * (double)( ymax - ymin + 2 ) + 4096,
+    /* (an outline of many points: a row's table of starting points may be */
+    /* taken from the pool, for a row with PVG_FT_FAR_STEPS cells at least) */
+    if ( outline->n_points >= PVG_FT_BUCKET_POINTS )
+      cells += fmin( (double)( ymax - ymin ), cells / PVG_FT_FAR_STEPS ) *
+               ( ( PVG_FT_ROW_BUCKETS * sizeof ( PCell ) + sizeof ( TCell ) - 1 ) / sizeof ( TCell ) );
+    return (size_t)fmin( cells * sizeof ( TCell ) + 3.0 * sizeof ( PCell ) * (double)( ymax - ymin + 2 ) + 4096,
                          (double)PVG_FT_MAXIMUM_POOL_SIZE );
   }
 
